     "<br><br>On Friday, November 9, 2012 3:45:17 PM UTC-6, Jim wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\">\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">Karl:</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">Can the below command to be rewritten to RETAIN only those ind below XO = 25 \r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">AND those ind above  XO =30?  \r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">The ind I am trying to EXCLUE has odd XO count that count falls into the XO 25-30 zone.\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">I need to keep the XO=zero individuals because they were not selectively genotyped\r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">and I need retain both SG (and not SG) phenotypes for subsequent QTL analyses. \r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">Unfortunately, I don’t know R well enough to make the below cmd do this. \r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">Your help on this would be appreciated. \r\n</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">OBJNAME &lt;- subset(OBJNAME, ind=(countXO(OBJNAME) &lt; 100))</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\">Jim</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:black\"> </span></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n</blockq